ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: red
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS: [Part B (the actual novel claim) is a Jensen-inequality identity: per-partition optimum >= shared-constraint optimum is DEFINITIONALLY non-negative, not a discovery — the only question is magnitude, and the model says ~0.46pp; Part A "agentic>reasoning" is fully explained by the trivial mechanism "workload with more shallow-saturating tokens gets more cheap exits under the SAME global tau" — no novel mechanism required, no conditioning needed; the sigmoidal c_k(L) + err_k(L)=base_err*(1-c_k(L)) construction mechanically guarantees that once global tau is tuned to the error budget, boilerplate already exits near its saturation depth, leaving only a thin Jensen sliver for per-class to capture — the ~0.5pp result is a direct algebraic consequence of the model's own functional form, not an empirical finding about LMs]
CONFOUNDS: [boilerplate fraction f in [0.2,0.35] is ASSUMED, not measured on real agent traces — the entire "realistic-f" anchor is unverified and f is the dominant lever in the sweep (delta_pp scales 0.31->0.64 across f); per-class saturation-depth gap (d_value - d_boiler) is a free parameter swept over {0.2,0.35,0.5} with NO empirical grounding — real LM intermediate-head confidence by token class is owed and could collapse the gap entirely or invert the boilerplate/value ordering; FLOPs/token = L/L_total ignores intermediate-LM-head compute, batched-exit raggedness, and KV-cache costs — these are precisely the overheads that killed naive early-exit in practice (CALM/LayerSkip papers); error model err_k(L)=base_err*(1-c_k(L)) ties errors to the SAME confidence signal used for the exit decision, which mechanically maximizes the apparent benefit of confidence-based exits — a real model where exit-confidence and prediction-correctness are imperfectly correlated would shrink BOTH global and per-class savings non-uniformly; matched-quality is enforced per-token, but agentic correctness is sequence-level (one wrong tool-call arg invalidates the call) — the right error budget is per-sequence, which tightens E and shrinks savings further]
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED: [sigmoidal class-conditioned confidence curve c_k(L)=sigmoid(a*(L/L_total - d_k)) — no real-model measurement that confidence-by-layer is sigmoidal OR that token classes separate by saturation depth at all; that boilerplate tokens are SHALLOWER-saturating than value tokens (could be the reverse — high-entropy free-text might saturate later than constrained-grammar tokens which are decided by syntax not semantics, but constrained-grammar tokens could equally be decided trivially at layer 0 by a single attention head — neither direction is established); that the matched-quality constraint is the right operating regime — production agentic systems may tolerate near-zero token error, pushing E -> 0 and erasing savings (tripped N3); that early-exit overhead is negligible (flagged in limitations but not modeled); that token-class identity is KNOWABLE at decode time without computing layers (per-class tau requires knowing the class BEFORE deciding to exit — circular unless class is derivable from prompt structure / state-machine position, which is unaddressed)]
STATISTICAL_GAPS: [the 5pp pre-registered bar is not justified — why 5pp and not 1pp or 10pp? a pre-reg bar this large makes N1 nearly tautological given the Jensen-floor mechanism the author already understood; reporting "0/90 clear 5pp" is the right honest result but the magnitude question (0.46pp) is reported with NO confidence interval / NO test against the null delta=0 — given struct_flop_sd <= 0.0043 the gap is statistically nonzero but practically negligible; only 5 seeds — fine for a deterministic-dominant analytic model but the variance partition (noise vs. tau-grid-resolution vs. seed) is not decomposed; no sensitivity analysis on the sigmoid slope a (only {6,10} tested) — the saturation-sharpness parameter directly controls how much per-class can win, and a sweep of 2 values is inadequate; no prior on whether real LM intermediate-confidence is well-approximated by ANY of these (a,d_k) settings — the model could be in a regime where per-class wins 10pp on real LMs, or 0pp; the L0 result is consistent with EITHER]
RATIONALE: Part B is a Jensen identity dressed as a hypothesis — the pre-reg honestly flags this ("existence of a gain is DEFINITIONAL, not a discovery"), the only question is magnitude, and the model returns the Jensen-floor magnitude (~0.5pp) that its own functional form makes nearly inevitable. The author has correctly executed an honest negative for Part B, reproducing the EXP-0006 grammar-region failure mode (CLAIM-0006 prior, this repo). Part A "held" is delivered by the global policy alone — i.e. NOT the claim's novel contribution; rebranding Part A as the deliverable is a goalpost shift. The prior-art owe (CALM Schuster22, Depth-Adaptive Elbayad20, SkipDecode23, LayerSkip24) is acknowledged but unresolved — none of these papers report a structure-aware-per-class threshold win over best-tuned global on agentic traces because the comparison is not common, which is suggestive that the community implicitly knows the Jensen sliver is small. RED because: (1) the novel knob (Part B) is dead at the pre-registered bar by the experiment's own honest verdict; (2) the analytic model's positive direction (>=0 delta) is definitionally guaranteed and cannot constitute evidence FOR the claim regardless of magnitude; (3) the un-measured assumptions (real f, real per-class saturation gap, intermediate-head overhead, sequence-level error budget, class-knowable-at-decode-time) are exactly the levers that could move the result, and all of them point AGAINST the claim under standard published findings on early-exit overhead; (4) no path from L0 to a HELD verdict is visible without first paying the owed real-model L1 measurement, which the limitations section concedes is the actual experiment. Honest negative; vote it as such.
